// EXPECT: E0242 — `xx` where nothing supplies a target type (ADR-0046 §2)
// A `:=` declaration infers its type *from* the initialiser, so there is no context to convert
// to. Deliberately not defaulted: an `xx` that picked a type would convert to something nobody
// wrote, which is the opposite of what a conversion is for. The help names `cast(T, x)`, because
// an explicit form exists and always will.
main :: () {
    n := 300;
    m := xx n;
}
